<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#peanut">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peanut</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as peanuts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The peanut is a legume native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> that spread worldwide after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian exchange</a>. Along with other New World crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a>, it was adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, where it could be grown on marginal land. These crops helped support the Qing Empire's dramatic population growth in the eighteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peanut.jpg" alt="Peanut" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arachis_hypogaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-163.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The genus Arachis is native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, east of the Andes, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. The cultivated peanut, Arachis hypogaea, arose from a hybrid between two wild species, thought to be Arachis duranensis and Arachis ipaensis. The first hybrid would have been sterile, but spontaneous chromosome doubling restored its fertility. Genetic analysis suggests the hybridisation happened only once, giving rise to a wild form found in a few places in north-western Argentina, and by artificial selection to the cultivated plant.</p><p>Domestication made the plant bushier and more compact, with a different pod structure and larger seeds. Cultivation spread from its centre of origin and formed further centres of diversity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, Ecuador, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. The oldest known remains of pods, about 7,600 years old, were found in Peru, where dry conditions preserve organic material; cultivation almost certainly began earlier in the moister centre of origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The peanut is a legume grown mainly for its seeds, which are held in pods that develop underground, a trait called geocarpy and unusual among legumes. After fertilisation a short stalk grows into the soil, and the fruit ripens there. Botanically the pods are not nuts. Like other legumes, peanuts harbour nitrogen-fixing bacteria in root nodules, which improve soil fertility and make them valuable in crop rotation.</p><p>The seeds yield oil and are eaten roasted, boiled or ground into peanut butter and sauces. Peanut sauces are used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> peanuts are made into chikki, a sweet brittle. The plant tops are used for silage and the oilcake left after pressing is fed to livestock. Between 1.4 and 2 per cent of people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> experience allergic reactions to peanuts, which can be fatal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Many pre-Columbian cultures, such as the Moche, depicted peanuts in their art, and cultivation was well established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> before the Spanish arrived. The conquistadors found the plant, called tlālcacahuatl in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, on sale in the marketplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. European traders later spread it across the tropics and subtropics. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> it substantially replaced the Bambara groundnut, another legume whose pods ripen underground.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> peanuts were introduced in the colonial period and grown as a garden crop; from 1870 they served as animal feed until human consumption grew in the 1930s. George Washington Carver promoted the peanut in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a>, where repeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> planting had depleted the soil. Marcellus Gilmore Edson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> patented a process for making peanut butter in 1884, and it became well known after the Beech-Nut company sold it at the St. Louis World's Fair in 1904.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peanut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This growth was aided by long internal peace and by New World crops, particularly maize, sweet potatoes and <b>peanuts</b>, which extended cultivation into previously marginal hill and upland territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They ate maize, squash, beans, sweet potato, manioc, achira and <b>peanuts</b>, with some fish, and grew cotton, coca, the San Pedro cactus and gourds.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nazca-culture">Nazca culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Sweet potato, maize, squash, beans, peppers, <b>peanuts</b> and pineapples were grown too, and the Taíno fished with nets, weirs and spears, hunted hutias, birds and iguanas, and kept fish and turtles alive in weirs until they were eaten.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century their numbers doubled, helped by maize, <b>peanuts</b>, sweet potatoes and potatoes from the Americas and new rice from Southeast Asia, and farmers had to work ever smaller holdings more intensely; “the population continues to grow, but the land does not”, the Qianlong Emperor remarked.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li><li><blockquote>…on a trumpet in tribute to the pitcher José Fernández, who had died in a boating accident that month, and in 2019 the Hartford Yard Goats, who had banned <b>peanuts</b> at their ground over allergies, chose new, nut-free words for the line about snacks by a fans' contest.</blockquote><a href="/songs/take-me-out-to-the-ball-game">Take Me Out to the Ball Game</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945 the Groundnut Scheme, a vast plan to grow <b>peanuts</b>, collapsed at great cost.</blockquote><a href="/state/tanganyika-territory">Tanganyika Territory</a></li></ul></section>
